  "title": "Soundscape descriptors and a conceptual framework for developing predictive soundscape models",
  "authors": "Aletta, F., Kang, J. and Axelsson, O., 2016",
  "central_proposition": "This paper proposes a conceptual framework for developing predictive soundscape models by identifying relevant soundscape descriptors and indicators, and outlining a systematic process for their measurement and modeling to facilitate soundscape integration into urban planning and design.",
  "concept_definitions": [
    {
      "name": "Soundscape",
      "definition": "[the] acoustic environment as perceived or experienced and/or understood by a person or people, in context (International Organization for Standardization, 2014)."
    },
    {
      "name": "Soundscape descriptors",
      "definition": "measures of how people perceive the acoustic environment."
    },
    {
      "name": "Soundscape indicators",
      "definition": "measures used to predict the value of a soundscape descriptor."
    },
    {
      "name": "Predictive soundscape model",
      "definition": "provides a means of predicting the value of a soundscape descriptor, and the blueprint for how to design soundscape."
    },
    {
      "name": "Noise Annoyance",
      "definition": "a multifaceted concept that chiefly covers immediate behavioural effects and evaluative aspects related to 'noise' (Guski, Felscher-Suhr, & Schuemer, 1999)."
    },
    {
      "name": "Pleasantness",
      "definition": "hedonic value of sound, perceived as pleasant or unpleasant."
    },
    {
      "name": "Quietness or tranquillity",
      "definition": "a 'tranquil space,' defined as a 'space that can facilitate a state of tranquillity' (Pheasant, et al., 2008)."
    },
    {
      "name": "Perceived affective quality",
      "definition": "a 2-dimensional soundscape model of perceived affective quality (e.g., Pleasantness–Eventfulness, or Calmness–Vibrancy)."
    },
    {
      "name": "Appropriateness",
      "definition": "whether or not a soundscape is appropriate for a place."
    }
  ],
  "argument_structure": "The paper first reviews existing soundscape descriptors, highlighting their strengths and weaknesses. It then introduces a three-step conceptual framework for developing predictive models: collecting soundscape data, characterizing the acoustic environment, and modeling their relationship. Finally, it discusses principles for selecting descriptors and calls for standardization within the soundscape community.",
  "mechanism_or_causal_logic": "The core causal logic is that human perception of the acoustic environment (soundscape descriptors) can be systematically predicted by measurable physical and perceived properties of the acoustic environment (soundscape indicators). This predictive relationship is established through a three-step process: 1) rigorous collection of subjective soundscape data, 2) objective characterization of the acoustic environment, and 3) statistical modeling to link these two domains, thereby enabling the design and planning of desired soundscapes.",
